<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brussels-Capital Region</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1989</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Brussels-Capital Region is one of the three regions of federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a>. Planned in the state reform of 1970 but only set up with its own parliament and government in 1989, it comprises the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and eighteen other municipalities. Officially bilingual in French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, it hosts the main institutions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and the headquarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brussels-capital-region.jpg" alt="Brussels-Capital Region" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Conde Sánchez · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Place_Bruselas_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
